Package: dmrfit
Date: 2026-10-06
Type: Package
Title: Scalable Bayesian Inference for Discrete Markov Random Fields
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R: c(person(given = "Giuseppe",
             family = "Arena",
             role = c("aut", "cre"),
             email = "g.arena@uva.nl",
             comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0001-5204-3326")))
Description: Estimation of discrete Markov random fields through the pseudolikelihood, for both Ising models
    (Ising (1925) <doi:10.1007/BF02980577>) and ordinal Markov random fields (Marsman et al. (2025)
    <doi:10.1017/psy.2024.4>). Point estimates, either maximum pseudolikelihood or maximum a posteriori, are
    obtained through a trust region optimization algorithm, with robust standard errors from the Huber-White
    sandwich estimator, for both full and constrained network structures. Bayesian inference corrects the
    pseudo-posterior through coordinate rescaling (Arena and Marsman (2026) <doi:10.48550/arXiv.2601.17205>), which matches its covariance to the sandwich covariance, and
    samples from it with the Fisher adaptive Metropolis-adjusted Langevin algorithm (Titsias (2024)
    <doi:10.48550/arXiv.2305.14442>). For comparison, the posterior based on the full likelihood can also be
    sampled, exactly for small networks or with the double Metropolis-Hastings algorithm (Liang (2010)
    <doi:10.1080/00949650902882162>). Savage-Dickey Bayes factors test each pairwise interaction, computed either
    by sampling importance resampling (Skare et al. (2003) <doi:10.1111/1467-9469.00360>) or from the posterior
    draws.
